WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:06.160
Hello! In today's Bug Bounty report explained&nbsp;
you will learn how it was possible to achieve&nbsp;

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:12.720
local remote code execution in playstation now&nbsp;
application due to insecure websocket server.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:19.120
The vulnerability was reported on hackerone&nbsp;
by Parsia Hakimian and he got $15,000 for it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:24.325
Links to the original report and to his twitter&nbsp;
are in the description. Enjoy! &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.169 --> 00:00:34.240
Playstation Now is a cloud gaming subscription service developed&nbsp;
by Sony. It allows streaming games from PS2, PS3 and PS4&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:39.000
on newer consoles and, most importantly for us, on&nbsp;
PCs.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:46.756
It has a desktop application that users open&nbsp;when they want to play a game. &nbsp;

00:00:47.465 --> 00:00:53.146
The main application&nbsp;
is called QAS and it's written in C++. 

00:00:53.704 --> 00:01:04.000
It also&nbsp;runs a local WebSocket server on port 1235.&nbsp;
But there is also another application - AGL.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:09.309
And by default QAS runs it with this URL&nbsp;
argument:

00:01:09.818 --> 00:01:18.327
It's written in Electron which uses chromium browser inside. It loads the website from&nbsp;
the URL and presents it to the user.

00:01:18.755 --> 00:01:25.840
I've mentioned&nbsp;&nbsp;that the main application runs the WebSocket&nbsp;
server. Let's now explain what it means.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:34.240
With HTTP it's always the browser sending a&nbsp;
request and the server can only send responses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:42.560
And WebSockets were created to allow browsers to&nbsp;
establish asynchronous connection with the server.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:48.273
It means that both sides can send&nbsp;
messages whenever they want.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.263 --> 00:01:56.960
However, there are security concerns because HTTP&nbsp;
traffic is protected by Same-Origin-Policy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:03.440
It's a mechanism that protects websites from&nbsp;
different domains from stealing each other's data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:09.520
But WebSocket connections do not&nbsp;
obey Same-Origin-Policy rules.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:15.840
And the WebSocket connection is initiated&nbsp;
by the following javascript code:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:26.240
When it gets executed, such HTTP request is being&nbsp;
sent. And in the Origin header the browser will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:34.720
put the domain from which this code was executed.&nbsp;
And it's the server's responsibility to verify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:42.960
if this origin is trusted or not. If it's valid,&nbsp;
the server responds with appropriate response&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:47.840
and the websocket connection is then&nbsp;
established and both sides can communicate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:53.650
But the Playstation Now application did not verify&nbsp;
the origin. &nbsp;

00:02:55.109 --> 00:03:04.000
So when a user opened the attacker's&nbsp;website while the PS Now application was running&nbsp;in the background, the attacker could write this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:09.840
javascript code and it would allow to establish&nbsp;
the connection with the local WebSocket server.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:18.993
It's because this code would be executed in the&nbsp;
user's browser meaning that the localhost refers&nbsp;to his machine.

00:03:20.546 --> 00:03:26.080
But just establishing a websocket&nbsp;
connection does not really have an impact. 

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:33.920
Let's&nbsp;see what we can do with it. Embedding untrusted&nbsp;
website inside Electron application can be very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:40.800
harmful but it's not always the case. It depends&nbsp;
on Electron security settings in the application.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:49.440
If you've watched my video about RCE in Microsoft&nbsp;
Teams, you know how Electron is built and what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:56.800
nodeIntegration security does. If you didn't I&nbsp;
highly recommend watching it. It's available in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:05.840
the cart right now, but I will remind those facts&nbsp;
anyway here. So Electron application is composed of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:11.099
Node main process and the renderer which is&nbsp;
a headless Chromium browser. &nbsp;

00:04:12.237 --> 00:04:18.800
And when nodeIntegration is disabled, there is a separation&nbsp;
between the main process and the renderer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:27.040
It prohibits the website opened in the renderer&nbsp;
from accessing Node.js API, because it could lead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:35.200
to remote code execution. Without it the website&nbsp;
opened in the renderer can't just import child_process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:44.320
and execute code. But the AGL application&nbsp;
used nodeIntegration = true setting which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:52.080
means that the website opened in the renderer can&nbsp;
do anything using Node.js API and this includes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:58.002
importing child_process and executing code
which is presented at the moment on the screen.

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:04.400
Let's now put this all together&nbsp;
and see how the full exploit looked.

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:11.776
Normally, messages sent via the WebSocket&nbsp;
connection looked like this: 

