We have a command, its parameters, the application that calls&nbsp;
the command and the target application. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:26.687
And one of the commands looked particularly&nbsp;
interesting for us. It was setUrl. 

00:05:27.811 --> 00:05:35.440
It made&nbsp;the target application open the provided website&nbsp;
and embed its content to present it to the user.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:44.720
By default, it was some site from Playstation&nbsp;
server but it was also possible to set this URL

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:51.840
to the exploit you've seen seconds ago.&nbsp;
So the full exploit would look like this:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.720 --> 00:06:00.080
The attack scenario is that the victim opens this&nbsp;
website while PS Now application is running in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:07.440
the background. Those first two lines establish a&nbsp;
connection with the local WebSocket server. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:16.163
Then, we send a WebSocket message with setUrl command&nbsp;
and the parameter is URL that points to our RCE&nbsp;exploit. 

00:06:17.542 --> 00:06:23.920
As the target application, we choose AGL&nbsp;
because this is the one that runs with Electron.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:32.160
And when our website is opened, the RCE gets&nbsp;
triggered and the attack is successful. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:38.240
The vulnerability was fixed and updated versions&nbsp;
of PS Now application are no longer exploitable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:44.480
The report was triaged as critical and&nbsp;
was rewarded $15,000 but the researcher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:49.120
is from Canada, so after taxes he&nbsp;
maybe got half of it to his pocket.&nbsp;&nbsp;

